{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who served as the Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century. His life was defined by complex political struggles within the Crusader states and his interactions with powerful figures like Baldwin IV. He inherited the county from his father Raymond II after the latter was assassinated by the Hashashin sect. Raymond III spent his early years under the regency of his mother Hodierna before assuming full control of his territories. The region of Tripoli faced constant threats from Muslim neighbors while also dealing with internal factional disputes among the Christian nobility.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond III became the ruler of Tripoli at a young age following the death of his father in 1152. He grew up during a turbulent period marked by the rise of Saladin and the fragmentation of Islamic power in the Levant. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he reached maturity. This period allowed him to develop diplomatic skills that would later define his career. He managed to secure alliances with neighboring Frankish lords and maintained the independence of his county against external pressures. His strategic location made Tripoli a crucial link between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and other Crusader holdings.\n\n*Relationship with Baldwin IV*\nThe reign of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem created a unique dynamic for Raymond III. The king suffered from leprosy yet remained a formidable military leader despite his illness. Raymond served as regent for Baldwin during the king's minority and later acted as a key advisor. Their relationship was often strained due to political maneuvering and succession disputes. Raymond supported the faction led by Raynald of Châtillon at times while also seeking to protect the kingdom from internal strife. He played a vital role in the nomination of Guy of Lusignan as Baldwin's successor which proved to be a controversial decision.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nThe final years of Raymond III were marked by the catastrophic Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was destroyed. He did not participate in this battle due to illness or political disagreement with Guy of Lusignan. Following the defeat he retreated to Tripoli to organize the defense of his remaining lands. The city of Tripoli managed to withstand sieges that saw other major cities fall to Saladin. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly after the battle of Hattin. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as it faced increasing isolation. His legacy remains a subject of historical debate regarding his loyalty and political acumen during the critical final decades of the Crusader states. The history of the region changed dramatically after his passing as the balance of power shifted irreversibly.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0033115083}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.06942511}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0062119984}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.821722}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.05141045}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.12514327}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.17238124}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.8484013}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0080436645}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.000658908}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.4305125e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.03939947}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.027741093}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.007682072}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -6.5565323e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -2.3841887e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.08832438}, {"token": " His", "logprob": -0.78660667}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.01059137}, {"token": " was", "logprob